*** README.orig	Thu Jul 13 09:05:00 1989--- README	Wed Jul 19 07:01:01 1989****************** 1,20 ****-   	This vesion of poker was written under early BSD 4.2 (DYNIX),-     with a UCB universe.- 	It is not guaranteed to work when compiled in the att universe -     (System V release 2) due to differences in curses.    	The file poker.h should be edited to define how the instructions      are to be displayed. /usr/ucb/more is used, but this can be altered,      and so must the path to the file poker_rules.  	! 	The title screen (with the implementors address) is displayed!     by the procedure title(), which is in p_scr.c  ! 	Most bugs have been removed, any that exist are in the hand!     recognition routines, and do not appear often. Anything that looks !     suspicious is probably just a design feature that you do not agree !     with, or is due to the compiler and universe used.  ! 	I make this statement because I will soon loose my account, and!     will not be able to help with queries. The code is however, fairly!     well structured, so it should not be too hard to modify.--- 1,54 ----  +     Systems+     -------+ + 	This game of poker, successfully compiles and works under the+     following three systems :+ + 	DYNIX 3.0.15 , this is early BSD4.2, with some BSD4.3 enhancements+ 		       running on a sequent machine.+ + 	and+ + 	Ultrix-32 V2.2 , on MicroVax.+ + 	and+ + 	HP-UX series 300 (with HPUXs300 #defined, courtesy of Erk+ 			  van Bronkhorst)+ +     There are, unfortunately, problems resulting from the use of curses, +     that prevent it working under att SYSV, at the moment.+ +     Implementor Notes+     -----------------+   	The file poker.h should be edited to define how the instructions      are to be displayed. /usr/ucb/more is used, but this can be altered,      and so must the path to the file poker_rules.+ + 	Poker.h also defines the speed of play, i.e. how long messages+     remain on the